<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.3.3: Solving More Complex Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02127</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.3.3: Solving More Complex Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>67fc95c1-a595-434a-87ce-e292326299a1</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001"> Solving an equation of the form \(a(x−h)^2=k\) is a similar process to the equations we have previously solved.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Notice that the quadratic term, \(x\), in the original form \(ax^2= k\) is replaced with \((x−h)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
        
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_05_06_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00004">Once the binomial is isolated, the square root property can be used. You can reason the solution by taking the square root and then appropriately isolating the variable using the two different square roots.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">Solve the following quadratic equations. Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">\(144=(n+1)^2\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00007">Enter the solution to the equation and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">\(n=11\) and \(n=-13\)</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Because 12 and −12 are the two numbers we can square to make 144 and \(n+1\) is the number being squared, we need \(n\) to be 1 less than 12 or −12. So \(n=11\) and \(n=-13\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00011">\((u−6)^2= 64\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00012">Enter the solution to the equation and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">\(u=14\) and \(u=-2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Since 8 and −8 are the two numbers we can square to make 64 and \(u−6\) is the number being squared, we need \(u\) to be 6 more than 8 or −8. So \(u=14\) and \(u=-2\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00016">\((n−5)^2−30=70\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00017">Enter the solution to the equation and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00019">\(n=15\) and \(n=-5\)</para>
          <para id="para-00020">To isolate the binomial, add 30 to both sides of the equation. Since 10 and –10 are the two numbers we can square to make 100 and \(n-5\) is the number being squared, we need \(n\) to be 5 more than 10 or –10. So \(n=15\) and \(n=-5\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00021">\(4(x+6)^2−5=31\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00022">Enter the solution to the equation and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00024"> \(x=-3\) and \(x=-9\)</para>
          <para id="para-00025">To begin isolating the binomial, add 5 to both sides of the equation. Then divide both sides of the equation by 4. Since 3 and –3 are the two numbers we can square to make 9 and \(x+6\) is the number being squared, we need \(x\) to be 6 less than 3 or –3. So \(x=-3\) and \(x=-9\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para id="para-00026">Solve the following quadratic equation. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00027">\((4x−3)^2+11=47\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00028">Enter the solution to the equation and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Two solutions: \(x=\frac{9}{4}\) and \(x=−\frac{3}{4}\).</para>
          <para id="para-00031">To isolate the binomial, subtract 11 from both sides of the equation. This leaves \((4x − 3)^2= 36\). When you take the square root of both sides, you end up with \(4x−3= \pm 6\). This leaves two equations to solve:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">\(4x−3=6\;\;\;\;\;\;\;4x−3=−6\)</para>
          <para id="para-00033">By solving each of them, you find the solutions \(x=\frac{9}{4}\) and \(x=−\frac34\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Video: Solving More Complex Quadratic Equations</title>
        <para id="para-00034">Watch the following video to learn more about how to solve a quadratic equation of the form \(a(x−h)^2=k\).</para>
        <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
          <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ece9009efec5cf2b9b57449f601cd71c89a38156"/>
        </media>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001"><para id="para-00035">Solve the following quadratic equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00036">\((x+3)^2−16=48\)</para></note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x - h)^2 = k\)</title>
        <para id="para-00037">We can use the square root property to solve an equation of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\) as well.</para>
        <para id="para-00038">Notice that the quadratic term, \(x\), in the original form \(ax^2=k\) is replaced with \((x−h)\).</para>
        <para id="para-00039">\(a {\style{color:red}x}^{2}=k \quad a{\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}x}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}h}{\style{color:red})}^{2}=k\)</para>
        <para id="para-00040">Like before, the first step is to isolate the term that has the variable squared. In this case, a binomial is being squared.</para>
        <para id="para-00041">Once the binomial is isolated, by dividing each side by the coefficient of \(a\), the square root property can be used on \((x−h)^2\).</para>
        <para id="para-00042">Solve \(4(y−7)^2=48\).</para>
        <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Isolate the quadratic term and make its coefficient 1.</para>
        <para id="para-00044"> So, divide both sides of the equation by 4.</para>
        <para id="para-00045">\(4(y−7)^2=48\) \((y−7)^2=12\)</para>
        <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the square root property on the binomial.</para>
        <para id="para-00047"> \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify the radical by pulling out the factors that are perfect squares.</para>
        <para id="para-00049"> Solve for \(y\). Rewrite the two solutions.</para>
        <para id="para-00050">\(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\) \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{(4 \cdot 3})\) \(y−7= \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\) \(y =7 \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\) \(y =7+2 \sqrt{3}\), \(y=7−2 \sqrt{3}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> Check the solutions.</para>
        <para id="para-00052">\(\begin{array}{rrr} 4(y-7)^{2}=48 &amp; 4(y-7)^{2}=48 \\ 4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}+}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(-2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(12)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(12)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 48=48 {\style{color:red}\checkmark} &amp; 48=48 {\style{color:red}\checkmark} \end{array} \)</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x-h)^2=k\)</title>
      <para id="para-00053">Solve the following quadratic equations.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
            <problem id="prob-00006">
              <para id="para-00054">\(3(a−3)^2=363\)</para>
              <para id="para-00055"> \(\frac{1}{2} (b+2)^2=200\)</para>
              <para id="para-00056">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00006">
              <para id="para-00057">Here is how to solve these quadratic equations of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\):</para>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
                <item>\(3(a−3)^2 =363\) \((a−3)^2=121\) \(a−3= \pm 11\) \(a−3=11\)      \(a−3=-11\) \(a= 14\) and \(a=-8\)</item>
              </list>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
                <item>\(\frac{1}{2}(b+2)^2=200\) \((b+2)^2=400\) \(b+2= \pm 20\) \(b+2=20\)      \(b+2=-20\) \(b=18\) and \(b=-22\)</item>
              </list>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
